build.host_notes:
Seed 4685 (mr_lib seed). The mr_lib preview of type/ReferenceType.java drew these hosts in order: TokenBuffer, DeserializationContext, BasicDeserializerFactory, ObjectReader, ObjectMapper, then BeanDeserializerBase as the 6th with n=6. That exhausts the six-host pool. All six are outside the package com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.type, so none can call ReferenceType's protected constructors. From outside that package, the only public ways to make a ReferenceType are:
- ReferenceType.construct, the facade itself, so calling it is recursion;
- ReferenceType.upgradeFrom, which copies a TypeBase base. Outside the package such a base cannot be built with the given superclass and interfaces: SimpleType.constructUnsafe has no bindings or supertypes, ResolvedRecursiveType has no supertypes, and SimpleType.refine returns null. It would also give a different hashCode, because base._hash is copied instead of name hash + refType.hashCode();
- the deprecated construct(Class, JavaType), which passes refType as anchorType and null as refType, so refType.hashCode() always throws an NPE.
No hop text was written, and no drawn host was skipped by hand.

build.reason:
toByte(String, byte) is Byte.parseByte, which can be written out exactly as a chain:
- NumberFormatException maps to the default;
- the sign and the empty / sign-alone cases;
- the per-sign magnitude limit and the narrowing;
- the digit loop in the gold.

The fault drops the gold's non-digit disjunct. Exactly one existing test catches it: testToByteStringI's "12.3" still gives the default, because 1193 exceeds the limit. The convention is pinned outside the gold by NumberUtils.toShort and toInt (same conversion, default for a non-digit String, with passing tests) and by NumberUtils.isDigits (per-character digit rejection, testIsDigits). All gates passed on the first build.

The facade's computation splits into a five-hop chain:
- hop 1 projects the map's values;
- hop 2 creates the LinkedHashMap and returns it;
- hops 3 and 4 pass the root and the map along;
- the gold holds the per-node walk rule.

Only one existing test catches the fault, for this reason. The gold treats a NAME as a leaf and does not enter its children. That changes nothing for any tree the parser builds: a NAME has children only as a var initializer, and an initializer can hold a VAR only inside a function. The one other test with a var inside a function is FoldConstantsTest::testVarLifting ('if(false)var a=function(){var b};'). There the function is an initializer, below a NAME, so the variant never reaches it and the test still passes. I logged every root that reaches the facade during the full suite: a function directly under the root occurs only in the trigger.

The convention is pinned outside the gold:
- SyntacticScopeCreator.scanVars does not examine a function's children, and SyntacticScopeCreatorTest::testFunctionScope checks this.
- NodeUtil.redeclareVarsInsideBranch, the production caller, documents 'not in an inner scope', and FoldConstantsTest::testVarLifting expects only 'var a' to be lifted.
- The facade's javadoc says 'excluding descent scopes'.
